{
  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Losartan, a well-known angiotensin II receptor antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) through its mechanism of action. The evidence suggests that Losartan, as part of the broader class of angiotensin II receptor antagonists, exerts its therapeutic effect by blocking the action of angiotensin II, a hormone that promotes vasoconstriction and sodium retention, both of which contribute to increased blood pressure and vascular remodeling. This mechanism is supported by the inclusion of Losartan in the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists class, which is directly linked to the treatment of CAD. The shared lowest common ancestor between Losartan and Telmisartan, as well as between Losartan and Valsartan, further reinforces the classification and the potential for similar therapeutic effects across these compounds.\n\nThe evidence from the visible paths indicates that Losartan may be repurposed for the treatment of CAD, as it is part of a class of drugs that have been shown to have beneficial effects on cardiovascular health. The presence of multiple pathways pointing to the same therapeutic outcome, combined with the established pharmacological mechanism of angiotensin II receptor antagonism, supports the hypothesis that Losartan could be a viable candidate for re-evaluation in the context of CAD. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of targeting angiotensin II signaling in the management of CAD, and suggests that Losartan may offer a novel therapeutic approach when considered in the broader context of cardiovascular disease.",
